== Chapter 11

#align(center)[#smallcaps(all: true)[IMPIETY OF ATTRIBUTING A VISIBLE FORM TO GOD.—THE SETTING UP OF IDOLS A DEFECTION FROM THE TRUE GOD.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[There are three main divisions in this chapter. The first refutes those who ascribe a visible form to God (s. 1 and 2), and answers the objection of those who, because it is said that God showed his presence by certain symbols, use this as a defense of their error (s. 3 and 4). Various arguments then follow, disposing of the stale objection drawn from Gregory’s saying that images are the books of the unlearned (s. 5-7). The second division of the chapter deals with the origin of idols or images, and with the adoration of them that the papists approve (s. 8-10). Their evasion is refuted (s. 11). The third division treats the use and abuse of images (s. 12), and whether it is expedient to have them in Christian churches (s. 13). The concluding part refutes the second Council of Nice \[Nicaea\], which most absurdly contends for images against divine truth, and even to the dishonor of the Christian name.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections.]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. God is set against idols, so that all may know he is the only fit witness to himself. He expressly forbids any attempt to represent him in a bodily shape.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Reasons for this prohibition from Moses, Isaiah, and Paul. The complaint of a heathen. It should put the worshipers of idols to shame.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. An objection drawn from various passages in Moses considered. The Cherubim and Seraphim show that images are not fit to represent divine mysteries. The Cherubim belonged to the tutelage of the Law.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[4\. The materials idols are made of amply refute the fiction of idolaters. Confirmation from Isaiah and others. The absurd precaution of the Greeks.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[5\. Objection: that images are the books of the unlearned. The objection answered: 1. Scripture declares images to be teachers of emptiness and lies.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[6\. The answer continued: 2. The ancient theologians condemn the making and worship of idols.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[7\. The answer continued: 3. The use of images condemned by the wantonness and harlot-like finery given them in popish churches. 4. The Church must be trained in true piety by another method.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[8\. The second division of the chapter. The origin of idols or images. Its rise shortly after the flood. Its continual progress.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[9\. Of the worship of images. Its nature. A pretext of idolaters refuted. Pretexts of the heathen. The mind of idolaters.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[10\. The evasion of the papists. Their agreement with the idolaters of old.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[11\. Refutation of another evasion or sophism, namely, the distinction of #emph[dulia] and #emph[latria].]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[12\. The third division of the chapter, namely, the use and abuse of images.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[13\. Whether it is expedient to have images in Christian churches.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[14\. The absurd defense of the worship of images by the so-called second Council of Nice. Sophisms or perversions of Scripture in defense of images in churches.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[15\. Passages brought forward in support of the worship of images.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[16\. The blasphemous expressions of some ancient idolaters, approved by not a few of the more recent, both in word and in deed.]

#v(0.8em)

1\. #smallcaps[As] Scripture, making allowance for the crude and gross intellect of man, usually speaks in popular terms, so whenever it means to distinguish the true God from false gods, it sets him especially against idols. It does this not because it approves what philosophers teach more elegantly and subtly, but so that it may better expose the folly, indeed the madness, of the world in its search for God, so long as each one clings to his own speculations. This exclusive definition, which we meet everywhere in Scripture, brings to nothing every deity that men make for themselves of their own accord, since God himself is the only fit witness to himself. Meanwhile, since this brutish stupidity has spread over the whole world, so that men long for visible forms of God and so make gods of wood and stone, silver and gold, or any other dead and corruptible matter, we must hold it as a first principle that whenever any form is assigned to God, his glory is corrupted by an impious lie. Accordingly, in the Law, after God has claimed the glory of divinity for himself alone, when he comes to show what kind of worship he approves and rejects, he immediately adds, “You shall not make for yourself a carved image, or any likeness of anything in the heavens above, on the earth below, or in the waters beneath” (Exod 20:4). By these words he curbs any license we might take to represent him in a visible shape, and he briefly lists all the forms by which superstition had begun, even long before, to turn his truth into a lie. For we know that the Persians worshiped the sun. As many stars as the foolish nations saw in the sky, so many gods they imagined them to be. Among the Egyptians, again, almost every animal was a figure of God.#footnote[The French adds, “voire jusques aux oignons et porreaux;”—they have gone even to onions and leeks. (Beveridge’s note.)] The Greeks, for their part, prided themselves on being wiser than the rest because they worshiped God in human form (Maximus Tyrius Platonic. Serm. 38). But God makes no comparison between images, as if one were more fitting and another less; he rejects, without exception, all shapes and pictures and other symbols by which the superstitious imagine they can bring him near to them.

2\. This is easily inferred from the reasons he attaches to his prohibition. First, it is said in the books of Moses (Deut 4:15), “So since you#super[+] saw no form of any kind on the day the #smallcaps[Lord] spoke to you#super[+] out of the fire at Horeb, be careful that you#super[+] do not act corruptly and make an idol for yourselves#super[+] of any form or shape,” and so on. We see how plainly God opposes his voice to all figures, to make us aware that all longing after such visible shapes is rebellion against him. Of the prophets it will be enough to mention Isaiah, who has the most to say on this subject (Isa 40:18; 41:7, 29; 45:9; 46:5), to show how the majesty of God is defiled by an absurd and unseemly fiction, when he who is incorporeal is likened to corporeal matter; he who is invisible, to a visible image; he who is a spirit, to a lifeless object; and he who fills all space, to a bit of paltry wood, or stone, or gold. Paul, too, reasons in the same way: “Therefore, being offspring of God, we should not think that the Divine Being is like gold or silver or stone, an image formed by man’s skill and imagination” (Acts 17:29). Hence it is clear that whatever statues are set up or pictures painted to represent God are utterly displeasing to him, as so many insults to his majesty. And is it any wonder that the Holy Spirit thunders such oracles from heaven, when he compels even blind and miserable idolaters to make a similar confession on earth? Seneca’s complaint, as Augustine gives it, De Civit. Dei, c. 10, is well known. He says, “The sacred, immortal, and inviolable gods they display in the cheapest and basest materials, and invest them with the forms of men and beasts; some mix the sexes and make a compound out of different bodies, giving the name of deities to objects which, if they were met alive, would be thought monsters.” Hence, again, it is plain that the defenders of images take refuge in a paltry, quibbling evasion when they claim that the Jews were forbidden to use them because of their proneness to superstition; as if a prohibition which the Lord founds on his own eternal essence and the constant course of nature could be restricted to a single nation. Besides, when Paul refuted the error of giving a bodily shape to God, he was addressing not Jews, but Athenians.

3\. It is true that the Lord at times showed his presence by certain signs, so that he was said to be seen face to face; but all the signs he ever used were well suited to his way of teaching, and at the same time plainly reminded men of his incomprehensible essence. For the cloud and smoke and flame, though they were symbols of heavenly glory (Deut 4:11), held back men’s minds as with a bridle, so that they would not try to penetrate farther. Therefore even Moses (to whom, of all men, God showed himself most intimately) was not allowed, though he prayed for it, to see that face, but received the answer that its brightness was too great for man (Exod 33:20). The Holy Spirit appeared in the form of a dove (Matt 3:16); but since it vanished at once, who does not see that in this symbol of a moment the faithful were taught to regard the Spirit as invisible, to be content with his power and grace, and not to call for any outward figure? God sometimes appeared in the form of a man, but this was a foreshadowing of the future revelation in Christ, and therefore it did not give the Jews the least pretext for setting up a symbol of Deity in human form. The mercy seat also (Exod 25:17, 18, 21), from which, under the Law, God showed the presence of his power, was so made as to indicate that God is best seen when the mind rises above itself in wonder: the Cherubim shaded it with outstretched wings, the veil covered it, and the remoteness of the place was in itself enough to hide it. It is therefore sheer madness to try to defend images of God and the saints by the example of the Cherubim. For what, I ask, did these figures mean, if not that images are unfit to represent the mysteries of God? They were made to cover the mercy seat with their wings, so shutting off the view of God not only from the eye but from every human sense, and curbing presumption. To this we may add that the prophets (Isa 6:2) depict the Seraphim, who were shown to them in vision, with their faces veiled. By this they indicate that the brightness of the divine glory is so great that even the angels cannot gaze on it directly, while the tiny rays of it that sparkle in the face of angels are hidden from our sight. Moreover, all men of sound judgment acknowledge that the Cherubim in question belonged to the old tutelage of the law. It is absurd, therefore, to bring them forward as an example for our age. For that period of childhood, if I may call it so, for which such elementary lessons were designed, has passed away. And surely it is a disgrace that heathen writers should be more skillful interpreters of Scripture than the papists. Juvenal (Sat. 14) mocks the Jews for worshiping the thin clouds and the deity of heaven. This he does perversely and impiously; yet in denying that any visible shape of Deity existed among them, he speaks more accurately than the papists, who babble that there was some visible image. When we see that the people again and again rushed out in feverish haste after idols, just like water gushing violently from a great spring, let us learn how prone our nature is to idolatry, so that we may not, by throwing the whole blame for a common vice on the Jews, be led away by vain and sinful enticements to sleep the sleep of death.

4\. The words of the Psalmist have the same aim (Ps 115:4, 135:15): “Their idols are silver and gold, the work of men’s hands.” From the material they are made of, he infers that they are not gods, taking it for granted that every human device concerning God is a dull fiction. He mentions silver and gold rather than clay or stone, so that neither splendor nor cost may win reverence for idols. He then draws a general conclusion: nothing is less likely than that gods should be formed of any kind of lifeless matter. At the same time he insists no less on the other point, that mortals are carried away by an excess of mad presumption when they, who hold their fleeting breath only on sufferance from moment to moment, dare to confer the honor of God on idols. Man is forced to confess that he is only the creature of a day#footnote[See Book 3 c. 9 s. 2. (Beveridge’s note.)], and yet he would have the metal he has deified regarded as God. Where did idols come from, if not from the will of man? There was good ground, then, for the heathen poet’s sneer (Hor. Sat. I. 8): “I was once the trunk of a fig tree, a useless log, when the craftsman, unsure whether he should make me a stool, and so on, chose rather that I should be a god.” In other words, an earthborn creature who breathes out his life almost every moment is able by his own device to confer the name and honor of deity on a lifeless trunk. But since that Epicurean poet, in indulging his wit, had no regard for religion, let us pass over his jeers and those of his fellows, and let the rebuke of the prophet sting us, indeed pierce us to the heart, when he speaks of the extreme folly of those who take a piece of wood to kindle a fire to warm themselves, bake bread, roast or boil meat, and out of the rest make a god, before which they bow down as suppliants (Isa 44:16). Hence the same prophet, in another place (Isa 40:21), not only charges idolaters as guilty under the law, but rebukes them for not learning from the foundations of the earth, since nothing is more incongruous than to reduce the immense and incomprehensible Deity to the stature of a few feet. And yet experience shows that this monstrous thing, though plainly contrary to the order of nature, is natural to man. It should be observed, moreover, that by the form of expression used, every kind of superstition is denounced. Being works of men, they have no authority from God (Isa 2:8, 31:7, 57:10; Hos 14:3; Mic 5:13); and therefore it must be held as a fixed principle that all modes of worship devised by man are detestable. The Psalmist (Ps 115:8) sets this madness in a still stronger light when he shows how help is begged from dead and senseless objects by beings who were given intelligence for the very purpose of knowing that the whole universe is governed by the power of God alone. But since the corruption of nature sweeps all mankind, collectively and individually, into this madness, the Spirit at last thunders out a dreadful curse: “Let those who make them be like them, and everyone who trusts in them.”#footnote[Calvin translates the words of the Psalmist as a curse, “Similes illis fiant qui faciunt ea;”—Let those who make them be like them. (Beveridge’s note.)] And it should be observed that the thing forbidden is #emph[likeness], whether sculptured or not. This disposes of the frivolous precaution of the Greek Church. They think they do admirably because they have no sculptured shape of Deity, while no one goes to greater lengths in the licentious use of pictures. The Lord, however, forbids any image of himself not only to be set up by a sculptor, but to be made by any artist whatever, because every such image is sinful and an insult to his majesty.

5\. I am not unaware, indeed, of the saying, now more than threadbare, “that images are the books of the unlearned.” So said Gregory;#footnote[See Gregory, Ep. ad Serenum Massiliens, Ep. 109. lib. 7; and Ep. 9 lib. 9; also Ep. 53. et 126. lib. 2., where Gregory, while wishing to excuse the worship of images, rather accuses it. (Beveridge’s note.)] but the Holy Spirit gives a very different verdict; and had Gregory learned his lesson on this matter in the Spirit’s school, he would never have spoken as he did. For when Jeremiah declares that “the wooden idol is a doctrine of vanities” (Jer 10:8), and Habakkuk (Hab 2:18) “that the cast image” is “a teacher of lies,” the general doctrine to be inferred is surely that everything about God that is learned from images is futile and false. If it is objected that the prophets’ rebuke is aimed at those who misused images for impious superstition, I admit it; but I add (what must be plain to all) that the prophets utterly condemn what the papists hold as an undoubted axiom, namely, that images serve in place of books. For they set images against the true God, as if the two were of opposite natures and could never be made to agree. In the passages I quoted a little earlier, the conclusion drawn is this: since there is one true God whom the Jews worshiped, visible shapes made to represent him are false and wicked fictions, and so all who turn to them for knowledge are miserably deceived. In short, if it were not true that all such knowledge is deceptive and counterfeit, the prophets would not condemn it in such general terms. This at least I maintain: when we teach that all human attempts to give a visible shape to God are emptiness and lies, we do nothing more than state #emph[verbatim] what the prophets taught.

6\. Moreover, let Lactantius and Eusebius#footnote[The French adds, “deux des plus anciens Docteurs de l’Eglise;”—two of the most ancient teachers of the Church. (Beveridge’s note.)] be read on this subject.#footnote[Lact. Inst. Div. lib. 1 c. 15; Euseb. Præf. Evang. lib. 3 c. 3, 4; also August. De Civitate Dei, lib. 4 c. 9, 31.] These writers take it as an indisputable fact that all the beings whose images were set up were originally men. In the same way Augustine plainly declares that it is unlawful not only to worship images, but to set them up to God. And in this he says no more than had been decreed long before by the Elibertine Council \[Council of Elvira\], whose thirty-sixth canon reads: “There must be no pictures used in churches: let nothing that is worshiped or adored be painted on walls.” But the most memorable passage of all is the one Augustine quotes in another place from Varro, and with which he expressly agrees: “Those who first introduced images of the gods both took away fear and brought in error.” If this were only Varro’s saying, it might perhaps carry little weight; yet it might well make us ashamed that a heathen, groping as it were in darkness, should have come to so much light as to see that bodily images are unworthy of the majesty of God, and this because they lessen reverent fear and encourage error. The thing itself shows that the saying was no less true than shrewd. But Augustine, while he borrows it from Varro, puts it forward as his own opinion. At the outset, indeed, he declares that the first errors into which men fell concerning God did not begin with images, but grew with them, as if new fuel had been added. Afterwards he explains how the fear of God was by this means extinguished or weakened, his presence being brought into contempt by foolish, childish, and absurd representations.#footnote[The French is “Pourceque la gloire de sa Divinite est vilipendée en une chose si sotte et lourde comme est un marmouset;”—because the glory of his Divinity is degraded into an object so silly and stupid as a grotesque figurine. (Beveridge’s note.)] I wish we did not experience the truth of this second remark so thoroughly. Whoever, therefore, desires to be instructed in the true knowledge of God must go to some other teacher than images.

7\. Let the papists, then, if they have any sense of shame, stop using the futile plea that images are the books of the unlearned, a plea so plainly refuted by countless passages of Scripture. And even if I granted the plea, it would not be a valid defense of their own idols. It is well known what kind of monsters they thrust on us as divine. For what are the pictures or statues to which they attach the names of saints, but models of the most shameless wantonness or obscenity? If anyone were to dress himself after their model, he would deserve the pillory. Indeed, brothels display their harlots more chastely and modestly dressed than churches display the images they intend to represent virgins. The dress they give the martyrs is no more seemly. Let the papists, then, show at least a little decency in decking out their idols, if they would give the least plausibility to the false claim that they are books of some kind of holiness. But even then we shall answer that this is not the way the Christian people should be taught in sacred places. Very different from these trifles is the doctrine in which God would have them instructed there. His command is that the doctrine common to all should be set forth there by the preaching of the Word and the administration of the sacraments, a doctrine to which little heed can be given by those whose eyes are carried to and fro gazing at idols. And who are the unlearned, whose crudeness allows them to be taught by images only? Just those whom the Lord acknowledges as his disciples; those whom he honors with a revelation of his heavenly philosophy, and desires to be trained in the saving mysteries of his kingdom. I confess, indeed, that as matters now stand there are not a few today who cannot do without such books. But where, I ask, does this stupidity come from, if not from the fact that they are cheated of the only doctrine that was fit to instruct them? The simple reason why those who had charge of the churches handed over the office of teaching to idols was that they themselves were mute. Paul declares that by the true preaching of the gospel Christ is portrayed and in a manner crucified before our eyes (Gal 3:1). What use, then, was there in setting up in churches so many crosses of wood and stone, silver and gold, if this doctrine were faithfully and honestly preached, namely, that Christ died to bear our curse on the tree, to atone for our sins by the sacrifice of his body, to wash them in his blood, and, in short, to reconcile us to God the Father? From this one doctrine the people would learn more than from a thousand crosses of wood and stone. As for crosses of gold and silver, it may be true that the greedy fix their eyes and minds on them more eagerly than on any word of God.

8\. As to the origin of idols, the statement in the Book of Wisdom (Wisd 14:15) has been received with almost universal consent, namely, that they began with those who paid this honor to the dead out of a superstitious regard for their memory. I admit that this perverse practice is very ancient, and I do not deny that it was a kind of torch by which mankind’s mad proneness to idolatry was kindled into a greater blaze. I do not, however, admit that it was the first origin of the practice. That idols were in use before that ambitious consecration of images of the dead, often mentioned by secular writers, became common is clear from the words of Moses (Gen 31:19). When he relates that Rachel stole her father’s idols, he speaks of the use of idols as a common vice. From this we may infer that the human mind is, so to speak, a perpetual forge of idols. There was a kind of renewal of the world at the flood, but before many years pass, men are making gods at will. There is reason to believe that in the holy patriarch’s lifetime his grandchildren were given to idolatry, so that he must have seen with his own eyes, not without the deepest grief, the earth polluted with idols: that earth whose iniquities God had lately purged with so fearful a judgment. For Joshua testifies (Josh 24:2) that Terah and Nahor, even before the birth of Abraham, were worshipers of false gods. Since the descendants of Shem fell away so quickly, what are we to think of the descendants of Ham, who had been cursed long before in their father? So indeed it is. The human mind, full as it is of presumptuous rashness, dares to imagine a god to suit its own capacity; and since it labors under dullness, indeed is sunk in the grossest ignorance, it puts emptiness and a hollow phantom in the place of God. To these evils another is added: the god whom man has thus conceived inwardly, he tries to embody outwardly. In this way the mind conceives the idol, and the hand gives it birth. That idolatry has its origin in men’s idea that God is not present with them unless he shows his presence to them in a carnal way appears from the example of the Israelites: “Come,” they said, “make us gods who will go before us. As for this Moses who brought us up out of the land of Egypt, we do not know what has happened to him!” (Exod 32:1). They knew, indeed, that there was a God whose mighty power they had experienced in so many miracles; but they had no confidence that he was near them unless they saw with their eyes a bodily symbol of his presence, as a proof of his actual government. They wanted, therefore, to be assured by the image that went before them that they were journeying under God’s guidance. And daily experience shows that the flesh is always restless until it has obtained some figment like itself, in which it may vainly comfort itself as a representation of God. Following this blind desire, men have, in almost every age since the world began, set up signs in which they believed that God was present before their carnal eyes.

9\. Once such a figment is formed, adoration follows at once: for as soon as men imagined that they saw God in images, they also worshiped him as being there. At length, their eyes and minds becoming wholly absorbed in them, they began to grow more and more brutish, gazing and marveling as if some divinity were actually before them. It is clear from this that men do not fall away to the worship of images until they have absorbed some grosser notion: not that they actually believe them to be gods, but that they imagine the power of divinity somehow dwells in them. Therefore, whether it is God or a created thing that is imaged, the moment you fall prostrate before it in veneration, you are to that extent bewitched by superstition. For this reason the Lord not only forbade the erection of statues to himself, but also the consecration of pillars and stones that might be set up for adoration. For the same reason, too, the second commandment has an added part concerning adoration. For as soon as a visible form is given to God, his power also is supposed to be bound to it. So stupid are men that wherever they figure God, there they fix him, and by necessary consequence they proceed to adore him. It makes no difference whether they worship the idol simply, or God in the idol; it is always idolatry when divine honors are paid to an idol, whatever the pretext. And because God does not will to be worshiped superstitiously, whatever is given to idols is so much robbed from him.

Let those who hunt for miserable pretexts in defense of the detestable idolatry, in which true religion has for many past ages been buried and sunk, take note of this. It is said that the images are not considered gods. Nor were the Jews so utterly thoughtless as not to remember that there was a God whose hand led them out of Egypt before they made the calf. Indeed, when Aaron said that these were the gods that had brought them out of Egypt, they boldly agreed, showing in no ambiguous terms that they wished to keep God, their deliverer, provided they saw him going before them in the calf. Nor should we think the heathen were so stupid as not to understand that God was something other than wood and stone. For they changed the images at will, but always kept the same gods in their minds; and they had many images of one god, and yet did not, because of the number of the images, imagine many gods for themselves;#footnote[The French is “Neantmoins ils ne disoyent point pour cela qu’un Dieu fut divisé;”—nevertheless, they did not therefore say that the unity of God was divided. (Beveridge’s note.)] besides, they daily consecrated new images without thinking they were making new gods. Read the excuses which Augustine tells us the idolaters of his time used (#emph[August. in Ps.] 113). The common people, when accused, replied that they did not worship the visible object, but the Deity that dwelt in it invisibly. Those, again, who had what he calls a more refined religion said that they worshiped neither the image nor any indwelling Deity, but by means of the bodily image beheld a sign of what it was their duty to worship. What then? All idolaters, whether Jews or Gentiles, were moved in exactly the way described. Not content with spiritual understanding, they thought that images would give them a surer and closer impression. Once this perverse representation of God was adopted, there was no end until, deluded again and again by new impostures, they came to think that God exerted his power in images.#footnote[French, “Ne vouloit monstrer sa vertu que sous les images;”—would only show his power under the form of images. (Beveridge’s note.)] Still, the Jews were persuaded that under such images they worshiped the eternal God, the one true Lord of heaven and earth; and the Gentiles, too, in worshiping their own false gods, supposed them to dwell in heaven.

10\. It is a shameless lie to deny that what was done of old in this way is also done in our day. For why do men prostrate themselves before images? Why, when they are about to pray, do they turn toward them as to the ears of God? It is indeed true, as Augustine says (in Ps 113), that no one prays or worships in this way, looking at an image, without being so moved that he thinks he is heard by it, or hopes that what he desires will be done for him by it. Why are such distinctions made between different images of the same God, that while one is passed by, or receives only ordinary honor, another is worshiped with the highest solemnities? Why do they wear themselves out with pilgrimages vowed to images when they have many like them at home?#footnote[The two last sentences in French are, “Car laissans là un crucifix, ou une image de leur nostre-dame, ou n’en tenans point grand comte, ils mettent leur devotion à un autre. Pourquoy est-ce qu’ils trotent si loin en pelerinage pour voir un marmouset, duquel ils ont le semblable à leur porte?”—For there, passing by a crucifix, or an image of what they call “Our Lady,” or making no great account of them, they pay their devotion to another. Why is it that they trot so far on a pilgrimage to see a grotesque figurine, when they have one like it at their door? (Beveridge’s note.)] Why at the present time do they fight for them to the point of bloodshed and slaughter, as for their altars and hearths, showing themselves more willing to part with the one God than with their idols? And yet I am not now listing the gross errors of the common people, errors almost infinite in number and in possession of almost all hearts. I am only pointing to what those profess who are most eager to clear themselves of idolatry. They say, “We do not call them our gods.” Nor did either the Jews or the Gentiles of old call them so; and yet the prophets never ceased to charge them with their adulteries with wood and stone, for the very acts which are done daily by those who wish to be considered Christians, namely, for worshiping God carnally in wood and stone.

11\. I am not unaware, however, and I have no wish to hide the fact, that they try to evade the charge by a more subtle distinction, which will be considered more fully later.#footnote[See below, s. 16, and ch. 12 s. 2. (Beveridge’s note.)] The worship which they pay to their images they cloak with the name of εἰδωλοδουλεία (#emph[idolodulia]), and deny that it is εἰδωλολατρεία (#emph[idolatria]). So they speak, holding that the worship which they call #emph[dulia] may be paid to statues and pictures without insult to God. Hence they think themselves blameless if they are only the #emph[servants], and not the #emph[worshipers], of idols; as if it were not a lighter matter to #emph[worship] than #emph[to serve]. And yet, while they take refuge in a Greek term, they contradict themselves very childishly. For since the Greek word λατρεύειν means nothing else than #emph[to worship], what they say amounts to confessing that they worship their images without worshiping them. They cannot object that I am quibbling over words. The fact is that they only betray their ignorance while they try to throw dust in the eyes of the simple. But however eloquent they may be, they will never prove by their eloquence that one and the same thing is two. Let them show how the things differ, if they would be thought different from the idolaters of old. For as a murderer or an adulterer will not escape conviction by giving some borrowed name to his crime, so it is absurd for them to expect that the subtle device of a name will clear them, if in fact they differ in nothing from idolaters whom they themselves are forced to condemn. But so far are they from proving that their case is different, that the source of the whole evil lies in a perverse rivalry with them, while with their minds they devise, and with their hands make, symbolic shapes of God.

12\. I am not, however, so superstitious as to think that all visible representations of every kind are unlawful. But since sculpture and painting are gifts of God, what I insist on is that both be used purely and lawfully, so that gifts which the Lord has bestowed on us for his glory and our good may not be perversely abused, indeed may not be turned to our destruction. We think it unlawful to give a visible shape to God, because God himself has forbidden it, and because it cannot be done without in some degree tarnishing his glory. And lest anyone should think we are alone in this opinion, those acquainted with the writings of sound theologians will find that they have always disapproved of it. If it is unlawful to make any bodily representation of God, it must be still more unlawful to worship such a representation instead of God, or to worship God in it. The only things, therefore, that ought to be painted or sculptured are things the eye can take in; the majesty of God, which is far beyond the reach of any eye, must not be dishonored by unseemly representations. Visible representations are of two kinds, namely, historical ones, which represent events, and pictorial ones, which merely display bodily shapes and figures. The former have some use for teaching or admonition. The latter, so far as I can see, are fit only for amusement. And yet it is certain that the latter are almost the only kind that have so far been displayed in churches. From this we may infer that their display was the result not of wise choice but of a foolish and thoughtless craving. I say nothing of the improper and unseemly form in which they are presented, or of the wanton license that sculptors and painters have indulged in here (a point I touched on a little earlier).#footnote[See above, s. 7. (Beveridge’s note.)] I only say that, even if they were otherwise faultless, they could be of no use in teaching.

13\. But, setting aside the above distinction, let us consider here whether it is expedient that churches should contain representations of any kind, whether of events or of human forms. First, then, if we give any weight to the authority of the ancient Church, let us remember that for about five hundred years, while religion was in a more flourishing state and a purer doctrine prevailed, Christian churches were generally free from visible representations.#footnote[See Preface, and Book 4, c. 9 s. 9. (Beveridge’s note.)] Their first admission as an ornament to churches, then, took place after the purity of the ministry had somewhat declined. I will not argue about how reasonable the grounds were on which they were first brought in; but if you compare the two periods, you will find that the latter had greatly declined from the purity of the times when images were unknown. What then? Are we to suppose that those holy fathers, if they had judged the thing useful and wholesome, would have allowed the Church to be without it for so long? Surely, because they saw very little or no advantage in it, and the greatest danger, they rejected it deliberately and on reasoned grounds, rather than leaving it out through ignorance or carelessness. Augustine attests this clearly in these words (Ep. 49. See also De Civit. Dei, lib 4 c. 31): “When images are placed in this way on high in seats of honor, to be looked at by those who are praying or sacrificing, though they have neither sense nor life, yet because they seem to have both, they affect weak minds just as if they lived and breathed,” and so on. And again, in another passage (in Ps 113), he says, “The effect produced, and in a manner forced on us, by the bodily shape is that the mind, being itself in a body, imagines that a body so like its own must feel as it does,” and so on. A little farther on he says, “Images are more able to bend an unhappy soul the wrong way, because they have mouth, eyes, ears, and feet, than to correct it, because they neither speak, nor see, nor hear, nor walk.” This indeed seems to be the reason why John (1 John 5:21) tells us to beware not only of the worship of idols, but of idols themselves. And from the fearful madness under which the world has so far labored, to the almost entire destruction of piety, we know too well from experience that the moment images appear in churches, idolatry has as it were raised its banner; because the folly of men cannot restrain itself, but at once falls away into superstitious worship. Even if the danger were less pressing, still, when I consider the proper purpose for which churches are built, it seems to me, I know not how, unworthy of their sacredness to admit any other images than those living and expressive ones which the Lord has consecrated by his own word: I mean Baptism and the Lord’s Supper, with the other ceremonies. These ought to hold our eyes more steadily and impress them more vividly than to need the aid of any images that human ingenuity may devise. Such, then, is the incomparable blessing of images: a blessing whose lack, if we believe the papists, can never possibly be made up!#footnote[The French is “qu’il n’y ait nulle recompense qui vaille un marmouset guignant à travers et faisant la mine tortue;”—that no compensation can equal the value of a grotesque figurine looking askance and twisting its face. (Beveridge’s note.)]

14\. Enough, I believe, would have been said on this subject, were I not in a way held back by the Council of Nice: not the celebrated Council which Constantine the Great assembled, but one held eight hundred years ago by the order and under the auspices of the Empress Irene.#footnote[The French is, “une mechante Proserpine nommée Irene;”—a wicked Proserpine named Irene. (Beveridge’s note.)] This Council decreed not only that images were to be used in churches, but also that they were to be worshiped. Everything I have said, therefore, is in danger of being badly undermined by the authority of this Synod. To tell the truth, however, I am not so much moved by this consideration as by a wish to make my readers aware of the lengths to which the madness was carried by those who were fonder of images than became Christians. But let us first dispose of this matter. Those who defend the use of images appeal to that Synod for support. But there is a refutation extant that bears the name of Charlemagne, and its style proves it to be a work of that period. It gives the opinions delivered by the bishops who were present, and the arguments by which they supported them. John, the deputy of the Eastern Churches, said, “God created man in His own image,” and from this inferred that images ought to be used. He also thought images were commended in the following passage: “Show me your face, for it is beautiful.” Another, to prove that images ought to be placed on altars, quoted the passage, “No one lights a lamp and puts it under a basket.” Another, to show the usefulness of looking at images, quoted a verse of the Psalms: “The light of your face, O Lord, is sealed upon us.” Another seized on this comparison: as the Patriarchs used the sacrifices of the Gentiles, so Christians ought to use the images of saints instead of the idols of the Gentiles. They also twisted to the same purpose the words, “Lord, I have loved the beauty of your house.” But the most ingenious interpretation was the following: “As we have heard, so also have we seen”; therefore God is known not only by hearing the word, but also by seeing images. Bishop Theodore was equally sharp: “God,” says he, “is wonderful in his saints”; and elsewhere it is said, “To the saints who are on earth”; therefore this must refer to images. In short, their absurdities are so extreme that it is painful even to quote them.

15\. When they treat of adoration, great stress is laid on the worship of Pharaoh, the staff of Joseph, and the pillar which Jacob set up. In this last case they not only pervert the meaning of Scripture, but quote what is found nowhere. Then the passages “Worship at his footstool”—“Worship in his holy mountain”—“All the rich of the people will entreat your face” seem to them very solid and fitting proofs. If someone wanted to make the defenders of images ridiculous by putting words into their mouths, could he make them utter greater and grosser absurdities? But to put an end to all doubt on the subject of images, Theodosius, Bishop of Mira \[Myra\], confirms the propriety of worshiping them by the dreams of his archdeacon, which he brings forward as gravely as if he had an oracle from heaven in hand. Let the patrons of images go now and press us with the decree of this Synod; as if the venerable Fathers did not bring themselves into utter discredit by handling Scripture so childishly, or tearing it apart so shamefully and profanely.

16\. I come now to monstrous impieties, which it is strange they dared to utter, and doubly strange that all men did not protest against with the utmost horror.#footnote[The French adds, “et qu’il ne se soit trouvé gens qui leur crachassent au visage;”—and that people were not found to spit in their face. (Beveridge’s note.)] It is right to expose this frantic and shameful extravagance, and so strip the worship of images of the gloss of antiquity in which the papists try to deck it. Theodosius, Bishop of Amorium, fires off an anathema at all who object to the worship of images. Another attributes all the calamities of Greece and the East to the crime of not having worshiped them. What punishment, then, do the Prophets, Apostles, and Martyrs deserve, in whose day no images existed? They afterwards add that if the statue of the Emperor is met with perfumes and incense, much more are the images of saints entitled to that honor. Constantius, Bishop of Constantia in Cyprus, professes to embrace images with reverence, and declares that he will pay them the worship of honor which is due to the life-giving Trinity: everyone who refuses to do the same he anathematizes and classes with Marcionites and Manichees. In case you think this the private opinion of one man, they all agree. Indeed, John, the Eastern legate, carried still further by his zeal, declares that it would be better to let a city be filled with brothels than to give up the worship of images. At last it is resolved by common consent that the Samaritans are the worst of all heretics, and that the enemies of images are worse than the Samaritans. But so that the play may not end without the customary #emph[Plaudite] \[Applaud!\], the whole concludes thus: “Rejoice and exult, you#super[+] who, having the image of Christ, offer sacrifice to it.” Where now is the distinction of #emph[latria] and #emph[dulia] with which they would throw dust in all eyes, human and divine? The Council unreservedly grants as much to images as to the living God.#footnote[See Calvin, De Vitandis Superstitionibus, where also see Resp. Pastorum, Tigurin. adver. Nicodemitas. See also Calvin, De Fugiendis Illicitis Sacris. (Beveridge’s note.)]
